{"id":"kor-t1-0010","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"count.two","domain":2,"text_native":"이","text_romanized":"i","text_english":"Two.","source_caption":"2","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Continuing the count.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0011","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"count.zero","domain":2,"text_native":"공 (gong) / 영","text_romanized":"yeong","text_english":"None, zero.","source_caption":"0","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Zero is the answer that gets misheard most often, because the word for nothing and the word for a small quantity are often unrelated.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0012","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"price.bill","domain":3,"text_native":"계산서 부탁합니다.","text_romanized":"gyesanseo butakamnida","text_english":"The bill, please.","source_caption":"The check, please.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Where the word for bill and the word for receipt are the same word and neither is 'account'.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0013","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"price.card-ok","domain":3,"text_native":"신용 카드 받으십니까?","text_romanized":"sinyong kadeu badeusimnikka?","text_english":"Is card payment accepted?","source_caption":"Do you accept credit cards?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The alternative to carrying cash, and the question that decides whether you are standing in a queue that moves.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0014","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"price.how-much","domain":3,"text_native":"얼마나요?","text_romanized":"eolmanayo?","text_english":"How much is this?","source_caption":"How much?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The most useful question in the tier, and the one people are most afraid to ask. It is not rude anywhere.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0015","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"price.too-cheap","domain":3,"text_native":"싼","text_romanized":"ssan","text_english":"It is cheaper in my country.","source_caption":"cheap","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Read as an accusation almost everywhere. Included because knowing that it is an accusation is worth more than being able to say it politely.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0016","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"price.too-expensive","domain":3,"text_native":"너무 비쌉니다.","text_romanized":"neomu bissamnida","text_english":"That is too expensive.","source_caption":"That's too expensive.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Used with the hand gesture that accompanies it. The gesture carries the meaning and the word confirms it.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0017","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"money.atm","domain":3,"text_native":"현금 자동 지급기가 어디에 있습니까?","text_romanized":"hyeon-geum jadong jigeupgiga eodie itseumnikka?","text_english":"Where is an ATM?","source_caption":"Where is an automatic teller machine (ATM)?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"Recognising rather than producing it: you will be shown the sign rather than expected to ask.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0018","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"money.change","domain":3,"text_native":"환전해 주시겠습니까?","text_romanized":"hwanjeonhae jusigetseumnikka?","text_english":"Please change this into local money.","source_caption":"Can you change money for me?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The word 'change' means both coins and alteration in several of these languages, and the wrong one is confusing.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0019","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"money.exchange-rate","domain":3,"text_native":"환율이 얼마입니까?","text_romanized":"hwanyuri eolmaimnikka?","text_english":"What is the exchange rate?","source_caption":"What is the exchange rate?","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"Often answered by pointing at a board rather than spoken.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0020","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"toilet.where","domain":12,"text_native":"화장실이 어디에 있습니까?","text_romanized":"hwajangsiri eodie itseumnikka?","text_english":"Where is the toilet?","source_caption":"Where is the toilet?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The most urgent question in this tier and the one every phrasebook leads with. In several languages the word is the only one a traveller needs for years.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0021","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"food.menu","domain":4,"text_native":"메뉴를 봐도 되겠습니까?","text_romanized":"menyureul bwado doegetseumnikka?","text_english":"The menu, please.","source_caption":"Can I look at the menu, please?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"One question covers a whole meal. Most phrasebooks waste slots on individual dishes instead.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0022","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"food.no-meat","domain":4,"text_native":"저는 돼지고기를 먹지 않습니다.","text_romanized":"jeoneun dwaejigogireul meokji anseumnida","text_english":"I do not eat meat.","source_caption":"I don't eat pork.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Saying it once at the start of the trip saves a refusal at the table, where it is awkward for both sides.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0023","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"food.water","domain":4,"text_native":"물","text_romanized":"mul","text_english":"Water, please.","source_caption":"water","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Refusing the water in a restaurant is a small discourtesy in several of these countries. This is how to ask for some instead.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0024","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"way.straight","domain":7,"text_native":"곧장 가십시오.","text_romanized":"gotjang gasipsio","text_english":"Go straight ahead.","source_caption":"straight ahead","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"Recognised rather than produced.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0025","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"way.turn-left","domain":7,"text_native":"왼쪽으로 도십시오.","text_romanized":"oenjjogeuro dosipsio","text_english":"Turn left.","source_caption":"Turn left.","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"Recognised rather than produced: you are the one being given directions far more often than giving them.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0026","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"way.turn-right","domain":7,"text_native":"오른쪽으로 도십시오.","text_romanized":"oreunjjogeuro dosipsio","text_english":"Turn right.","source_caption":"Turn right.","register":"neutral","direction":"understand","why":"Recognised rather than produced.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0027","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"stay.checkout","domain":5,"text_native":"체크 아웃하고 싶습니다.","text_romanized":"chekeu autago sipseumnida","text_english":"I would like to check out, please.","source_caption":"I want to check out.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Said the morning before leaving, so the bill is not waiting at the desk while luggage is being gathered.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0028","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"move.destination","domain":6,"text_native":"저기에 데려가 주십시오.","text_romanized":"jeogie deryeoga jusipsio'","text_english":"Please take me there.","source_caption":"Take me there, please.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Shown with a map or a written address while saying it. 'There' is the word the pages use; 'this address' is in none of them, and a slot phrased from imagination rather than from a caption is a slot that can never be filled.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0029","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"health.doctor","domain":9,"text_native":"의사가 필요합니다.","text_romanized":"uisaga piryohamnida","text_english":"I need a doctor.","source_caption":"I need a doctor.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Short enough to say to anyone, which is the point — a phrase you cannot finish is a phrase that arrives too late.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0030","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"health.hurt","domain":9,"text_native":"상처를 입었습니다.","text_romanized":"sangcheoreul ibeotseumnida","text_english":"I have been injured.","source_caption":"I've been injured.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Declares the situation before being asked. Several of these languages change tense or register on the word for injury, and the flat version sounds like a complaint.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0031","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"help.dont-touch","domain":10,"text_native":"만지지 마십시오! , 만지지 마! (manjiji ma!, informal)","text_romanized":"manjiji masipsio!","text_english":"Do not touch me.","source_caption":"Don't touch me!","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Out loud and to a person, not shouted generally. Several of these languages treat it as an accusation, so it is a sentence to use deliberately.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0032","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"help.emergency","domain":10,"text_native":"응급 상황입니다.","text_romanized":"eunggeup sanghwang-imnida","text_english":"It is an emergency.","source_caption":"It's an emergency.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Changes how the next thirty seconds go. Two words, and in several of these languages it reaches a different set of responses than a request would.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0033","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"help.leave-me-alone","domain":10,"text_native":"혼자 내버려 두십시오.","text_romanized":"honja naebeoryeo dusipsio","text_english":"Please leave me alone.","source_caption":"Leave me alone.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Said firmly and quietly. Raising the volume is what turns it from a boundary into an incident in several of these cultures.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0034","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"help.lost","domain":10,"text_native":"길을 잃었습니다.","text_romanized":"gireul ireotseumnida","text_english":"I am lost.","source_caption":"I'm lost.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Asking a passer-by for help is a separate skill from reading a sign, and it uses different vocabulary in every one of these languages.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0035","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"help.phone","domain":10,"text_native":"당신의 전화기를 사용해도 되겠습니까?","text_romanized":"dangsinui jeonhwagireul sayonghaedo doegetseumnikka?","text_english":"Can I use your phone?","source_caption":"Can I use your phone?","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"The most common thing a traveller needs that a phone provides, and asking a stranger for it is usually granted.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0036","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"help.police","domain":10,"text_native":"경찰을 부르겠습니다!","text_romanized":"gyeongchareul bureugetseumnida","text_english":"Please call the police.","source_caption":"I'll call the police.","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Usually needs a phone, and in most places a local number rather than an international one.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
{"id":"kor-t1-0037","lang":"kor","tier":1,"slot":"help.thief","domain":10,"text_native":"서라! 도둑이야!","text_romanized":"seora! dodugiya","text_english":"Stop, thief!","source_caption":"Stop! Thief!","register":"neutral","direction":"say","why":"Loud, and aimed at a crowd rather than at one person. Included because the vocabulary exists and because it is the sentence that makes people nearby move.","exchange_id":null,"exchange_turn":null,"caution":null,"source":{"class":"curated","id":"Wikivoyage: Korean phrasebook","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Korean_phrasebook","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","external_id":null,"author":null},"failure_flags":[]}
